# SECTION 05 — ARCHITECTURAL CONTEXT

## Architectural Context

The Institutional Evolution Architecture is the constitutional layer responsible for governing continuous improvement across the entire COREI ecosystem.

Unlike previous phases that define *what* COREI is, the Evolution Architecture defines *how* COREI continuously becomes better.

It operates across every architectural layer without replacing ownership.

Each layer remains responsible for its own operation.

The Evolution Architecture continuously evaluates institutional effectiveness.

---

## Evolution Architecture Layers

The Evolution Architecture shall be organised into eight institutional layers.

---

### Layer 1 — Observation

Continuously observe institutional operation.

Observation includes:

* operator behaviour
* workspace usage
* capability utilisation
* workflow execution
* cognitive recommendations
* infrastructure behaviour
* engineering operations
* institutional outcomes

Everything measurable becomes observable.

---

### Layer 2 — Measurement

Transform observations into objective measurements.

Measurements include:

* workflow duration
* investigation efficiency
* execution quality
* response times
* operational interruptions
* workspace utilisation
* cognitive effectiveness
* architectural health

Institutional improvement begins with measurement.

---

### Layer 3 — Assessment

Evaluate measurements against institutional objectives.

Assessment includes:

* operational efficiency
* engineering quality
* governance compliance
* user experience
* architectural maturity
* institutional consistency

Assessment identifies strengths and weaknesses.

---

### Layer 4 — Insight

Generate institutional improvement opportunities.

Examples include:

* workflow bottlenecks
* capability gaps
* redundant actions
* repetitive tasks
* operator fatigue
* knowledge deficiencies
* architectural complexity

Insights become improvement candidates.

---

### Layer 5 — Planning

Convert validated insights into governed improvement plans.

Planning includes:

* priority
* impact
* implementation strategy
* dependencies
* institutional risks
* expected outcomes

Improvement becomes intentional.

---

### Layer 6 — Implementation

Govern institutional implementation.

Changes include:

* capability enhancements
* workspace refinements
* workflow optimisation
* cognitive improvements
* engineering upgrades
* architectural evolution

Implementation follows constitutional governance.

---

### Layer 7 — Validation

Validate institutional outcomes.

Validation verifies:

* objectives achieved
* operator benefit
* performance improvement
* governance compliance
* architectural consistency

Improvement must be measurable.

---

### Layer 8 — Institutional Learning

Successful improvements become institutional standards.

Knowledge enters:

* playbooks
* documentation
* architectural guidance
* governance standards
* engineering practices

Institutional learning becomes cumulative.

---

